fied automated logging framework for AI compliance is common, mundane. Many labs do logging middleware. So originality = 1? Common mundane boring. Evidence: standard middleware telemetry is a common engineering solution already expressed in industry.\n\napplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt directly satisfies mandatory technical requirements of Article 12(1) and 12(2). So solves identified problem. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nThis is logging to meet mandate. If implemented, would satisfy. Reasonable. So 4.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\nYes. Standard logging. Score 4.\n\nimplementability: degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n\nDeveloping middleware API integrated into all high-risk models, immutable database... That's significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. Might be 2? Or 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" could fit. Developing unified framework across all projects is significant engineering effort. Probably significant change, expensive. So implementability = 2? Let's think: It requires engineering team develop middleware API intercept system calls... That's non-trivial but feasible. Maybe 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. The anchor says 4 = easy low cost or non-radical changes. This is radical-ish. I think 3.\n\nLet's pick implementability 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <su