ptures timestamps metadata automatically during training/inference. Article 12 requires logs for events relevant to risk etc. Timestamps metadata may be insufficient for specific items like reference database etc. But general. Probably 3 = contributes to solution, partial. Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources. Unified library automates event recording. It will solve logging mandate largely. Effectiveness 4? \"reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability\". It will automate recording, prevent waste. Might be reasonable. I'd say 3 = contributes, partial solution because need specific data fields per Article 12(3). But maybe still solves.\n\nLet's pick 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial? Could be 4. Let's choose 3 to be safe? The proposal captures timestamps and metadata automatically. That addresses automatic recording requirement. It doesn't explicitly mention risk events, post-market monitoring etc. Might be partial.\n\nI'll give effectiveness = 3.\n\nEvidence: It automates generic timestamp and metadata capture which helps compliance but does not guarantee capture of Article 12(2)(a)-(c) specific events or Annex III details.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\nThis is directly about logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The library directly targets the mandate to technically allow automatic recording of events over lifetime.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking...\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes relationship e.g., logging woven into science\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nUnified library introduces new tool but treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That is level 2.\n\nEvidence: It adds a shared software tool for logging while keeping compliance framed as a separate logging practice rather than reshaping R&D.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified compliance logging library is fairly common engineering practice. Might be common mundane. Score 1? Or 2 interesting? Shared library to avoid duplication is common. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Centralized logging libraries are a standard industry practice and not surprising in AI labs.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nFormat: need <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably each on separate lines.\n\nLet's produce JSON? The prompt says return rating... For each subscale give \"..._evidence\" first then \"...\". Could be plain text.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid P2600\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nFollow order.\n\n",
