ges =4\nsome changes reasonably feasible =3\nsignificant change expensive difficult but not impossible =2\ntotally infeasible =1\n\nDeveloping middleware layer across diverse hardware clusters, mandatory library. That's significant change, expensive/difficult. Probably 2? Could be 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. Might be significant change. Let's say 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Evidence: Building a unified middleware across heterogeneous clusters requires substantial engineering effort and integration work.\n\nacceptability (1-4): common strategies violate no norms =4\nsomewhat uncommon... =3\noffends sensibilities somewhat =2\nradically violates =1\n\nUnified logging mandatory library is acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 unreasonable/unlikely\n1 solves unrelated problem\n\nWill this solve logging mandate? It ensures consistent automatic event recording, captures timestamps, input references, system states. Article 12 requires logging events relevant for identifying risk, post-market monitoring, monitoring operation, and for point 1(a) Annex III specific items like period of each use, reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons.\n\nProposal mentions timestamps, input references, system states automatically across hardware clusters. It does not explicitly mention recording period of each use start/end, reference database against which input checked, input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. Might be incomplete but generally addresses. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial? Could be 4 if solves problem without regard workability. It says single source of truth for system behavior throughout lifetime. Seems it would solve logging mandate. Maybe 3 because doesn't explicitly guarantee compliance with specific Annex III items like natural persons identification. Might still be 4.\n\nLet's interpret: effectiveness = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). The proposal seems to solve it. I'd give 3? Hmm.\n\nIt prevents researchers wasting hours building bespoke non-compliant logging. That suggests compliance.\n\nI think effectiveness 3 (contribute partial) or 4. The requirement includes specific items for point 1(a) Annex III. Proposal mentions timestamps, input references, system states. No mention of reference database, identification of natural persons. So maybe incomplete. So 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation\n1 intervention not stated...\n\nThis clearly applies: do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. So 4.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be plain text lines.\n\nI'll produce structured output with id and fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2335\noriginality_evidence: ... \noriginality: 1\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure sentence ends with period.\n\nProceed.",
