iolate no norms\nProposal is standard compliance infrastructure, no violation. So 4.\n\nNeed format: echo id. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOutput likely JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably produce list.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P3010\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\napplicability_evidence: The middleware directly targets Article 12's mandatory automatic event logging for high-risk AI systems to meet compliance. => applicability 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It will capture timestamps, input matches and data references but does not explicitly ensure logging of risk-triggering events, post-market monitoring data, or verifier identities required for Annex III systems. => effectiveness 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces a new shared logging tool and process but still treats compliance as a separate logging practice rather than redefining R&D-compliance relations. => paradigm_relatedness 2\n\noriginality_evidence: A universal plug-and-play logging layer for regulated AI is a common, mundane engineering response expected from experienced evaluators. => originality 1\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a standardized, architecture-agnostic middleware that integrates with existing training and inference pipelines requires substantial DevOps engineering and coordination. => implementability 3? Wait evidence says substantial -> maybe 2. Hmm conflicting. Let's keep implementability 2 to match evidence of significant change. Need consistent.\n\nLet's choose implementability 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult.\n\nEvidence: Building a standardized architecture-agnostic middleware across all high-risk models is a significant engineering effort with notable cost. => implementability 2\n\nacceptability_evidence: Shared logging infrastructure for regulatory compliance is a common strategy that violates no legal, social or business norms. => acceptability 4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
