n into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis is a dedicated verification interface with immutable ledger. Introduces new tools/process but still treats compliance as logging practice. Not changing relationship fundamentally. Slightly paradigm stretching? Probably 2. Could be 1 = standard compliance. But immutable ledger is somewhat new element. I think 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nHuman-in-the-loop audit trail with sign-off portal is common, mundane. Maybe 1 or 2. Common compliance practice. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nThus scores: acceptability 4, implementability 3, effectiveness 3, applicability 4, paradigm_relatedness 2, originality 1.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON output? Instruction says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably list.\n\nWill produce:\n\nid: P2268\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The proposal implements identity logging and sign-off as explicitly required by Article 12(3)(d) and Recital 71, aligning with legal and institutional accountability norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a mandatory authentication portal and immutable ledger requires development and integration but is a reasonably feasible IT control for an R&D lab. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It directly satisfies the Annex III human-verificatio