d do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, but only partial). Proposal addresses specific sub-set. It does not address general logging for all high-risk systems, nor post-market monitoring etc. So contributes partially.\n\nProbably effectiveness = 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). The proposal does X to get Y logging for Annex III biometric. That's directly related to stated problem of meeting logging mandate. Yes. Might be 4.\n\nBut maybe applicability =4 because it directly addresses explicit record-keeping mandates found in Article 12 paragraph 3. That is exactly the problem.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is specialized toolset for compliance logging. Introduces new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements (dedicated interface, queryable format) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's level 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination 2 = interesting 1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nBuilding a dedicated audit module for 