        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence.\n\nProposal P2121: Regulatory Impact Resource Allocation Review. Quarterly audit of project portfolio to identify which specific systems fall under high-risk categories and require Article 12 logging. Leadership team will review upcoming research milestones against AI Act's Annex III to allocate dedicated budget for compliance overhead early in cycle. Ensures we do not encounter sudden unfunded costs or project delays during final stages.\n\nRate subscales:\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources...\n\nProposal is about auditing portfolio to identify high-risk systems and allocate budget for compliance overhead early.\n\nDoes it solve identified problem directly related? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This proposal addresses resource allocation for compliance, not technical logging implementation. It applies: identifying which systems need Article 12 logging. That's directly related to mandate. Applicability 4? \"solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\". Does it solve logging mandate? It allocates budget for compliance overhead. It's about meeting mandate via planning. Might be applicability 4.\n\nEvidence: The quarterly audit directly targets identifying systems needing Article 12 logs and budgeting compliance overhead.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Will it solve logging mandate? It helps avoid unfunded costs/delays but doesn't actually implement logging. It's partial solution. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial.\n\nEvidence: Allocating budget early reduces delays but does not itself create logs or change R&D practices required by Article 12.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis is portfolio audit for resource allocation. That's standard compliance management. Likely paradigm preserving? Or slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new process of quarterly audit against Annex III. That is new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a portfolio review process but keeps R&D and compliance separate, treating logging as an overhead to budget.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\nCommon? Quarterly audit of portfolio against regulation is common. Originality 1 = common mundane boring. Could be 1.\n\nEvidence: Portfolio risk triage and early budgeting for regulatory overhead is a common management practice in regulated R&D.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms. This is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Internal budgeting audits for legal compliance are normal business practice and raise no legal or sensibility concerns.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes reasonably feasible\nProbably 3? Quarterly audit requires leadership time, mapping to Annex III. Might be some changes but reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Or 4 easy? Might be 3.\n\nThe anchor: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Quarterly audit is moderate change. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Setting up a quarterly review and budget reallocation requires process design and leadership time but uses existing governance structures.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? The instruction says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" . Might be plain lines.\n\nProbably produce:\n\nid: P2121\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nLet's do that.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure order: applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide scores.\n\nI think applicability = 4. Effectiveness = 3. Paradigm_relatedness = 2? Could be 1? Hmm slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. It does introduce audit process. So 2.\n\nOriginality = 1.\n\nAcceptability = 4.\n\nImplementability = 3 (or 4?). I'll choose 3.\n\nLet's output.",
