eware, removes burden. Does not change relationship? It says seamless compliance without interrupting creative flow. That's still treating compliance as separate but automated. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\napplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related... do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Stated problem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal does X (middleware) to get Y (automatic logs, compliance). That's directly related. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree will solve problem. 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Will automatic capture events timestamps input-output pairs solve logging mandate? Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of use etc. Middleware captures system events timestamps input-output. Might be partial? It captures generally but may not capture specific requirements like reference database against which input checked, identification of natural persons involved in verification... For point 1(a) Annex III systems need those specific items. Proposal says for all high-risk prototypes, captures system events timestamps input-output pairs. Could be reasonable and will solve main problem. Maybe partial? It ensures seamless compliance without interrupting creative flow. Probably 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial. Might be 4? It claims ensures seamless compliance. Hmm.\n\nGiven requirement includes specific logging capabilities for biometric systems etc. Middleware may need customization. I'd say it contributes but not fully guarantee all requirements. Could be 3.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strateg