        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence.\n\nProposal: Strategic Compliance Resource Reallocation. Budgetary shift dedicates specific portion of manpower and compute credits exclusively to regulatory engineering. Sunset three low-impact legacy projects to redirect those specific funding streams toward building traceability frameworks required by Act. It ensures essential compliance work does not cannibalize budgets of most ambitious and high-risk scientific pursuits.\n\nRate relevance effectiveness, applicability; novelty originality, paradigm_relatedness; workability implementability, acceptability.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDoes reallocation solve logging mandate? It provides resources for traceability frameworks. That's reasonable and will contribute to solution? Might be partial. Effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does it solve? It ensures compliance work does not cannibalize budgets... Building traceability frameworks required by Act. Seems it will help meet mandate. Might be full solution? It provides resources but doesn't specify technical logging. Could be considered reasonable and will contribute to solution, not necessarily solve fully. Could argue 4: if you could reallocate, you would solve resource constraint. Hmm.\n\nAnchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of the problem (it helps, but it is only a partial solution).\n\nI think reallocation alone doesn't guarantee technical logging capability; it's enabling. Probably contributes. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Reallocation to build traceability frameworks required by Act -> directly related. So 4.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nStrategic compliance resource reallocation, sunset low-impact projects to fund compliance. Is that common? Probably common budgeting. Might be mundane. Common business practice. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nReallocation to dedicated regulatory engineering, sunsetting projects. Still treats compliance as separate budget. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new element of dedicated resource pool. But still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably 2? Or 1? Standard compliance practice is reallocate resources. Hmm. I'd say paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged? Actually they sunset projects, changes. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools... processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That fits. So 2.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. 4 = easy low cost or non-radical. 3 = some changes or reasonably feasible. 2 = significant change or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. 1 = totally infeasible.\n\nSunsetting three projects and reallocating manpower/compute credits is significant change, maybe difficult politically. Could be reasonably feasible? Might be significant change. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Or 2. Hmm budget shift with sunsetting legacy projects likely requires management approval, but doable. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. 3 = somewhat uncommon... 2 = offends sensibilities somewhat. 1 = radically violates laws...\n\nSunsetting low-impact legacy projects to fund compliance is acceptable business practice. Might be 4.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each.\n\nProvide output format: echo id, then for each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P2042\neffectiveness_evidence: ... effectiveness: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure order: effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability\n\nReturn rating.\n\n",
