y common practice. Probably common, mundane. So originality =1? Common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting. Hmm standardizing logging is typical. Might be common. I'd give 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 = paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nProposal introduces tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. It standardizes. That's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Proposal provides unified logging to meet Article 12 logging mandate. Yes directly applies. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability. Unified framework records timestamps and input references... Does it cover all requirements? Article 12 requires recording events relevant for risk, post-market monitoring etc., and for systems in Annex III point 1(a) need period of each use, reference database, input data match, identification persons. Proposal mentions timestamps and input references into immutable ledger. Might be partial. Could solve main problem? Possibly contributes but not fully guarantee all event types. Hmm effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial. Or 4? It says provides consistent interface for all high-risk systems to record operational logs. Automatically records timestamps and input references. Might miss risk identification etc. Could be partial. I'd say 3.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each then score.\n\nOutput format: rating of proposal echoing id. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\"... Could output as lines.\n\nProbably produce:\n\nid: P2410\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nLet's comply.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Building a shared logging library to meet Article 12 is standard compliance practice that violates no legal or social norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Developing a shared API and template integration requires coordinated infrastructure work but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Centralized logging libraries and project-template integration are common, mundane engineering practices in regulated software. Score 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal adds a reusable tooling layer but keeps compliance as separate logging practice without changing how R&D is conducted. Score 2.\n\nApplicability evidence: The framework directly targets the mandate to automatically record events for high-risk AI systems under Article 12. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Standardized logging will contribute to traceability and post-market monitoring but does not by itself guarantee capture of all Article 12(a)-(c) risk-related events. Score 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
