y implemented. Establish cross-functional review board quarterly audit, produce compliance map. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Might need process. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. It's standard governance. So 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal introduces classification process and board. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements but relationship remains compliance separate? It does change relationship somewhat by weaving risk assessment into funding decisions. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nProposal is common risk-based classification for regulatory compliance. Common, mundane. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality. Give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... give ... Provide probably lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2861\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The framework directly classifies projects by AI Act risk to determine which logging requirements apply, matching the lab’s need to decide how to meet Article 12.\napplicability:4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It optimizes resource use by avoiding over-engineering for low-risk work but does not itself build the automatic logging capabilities required for high-risk systems.\neffectiveness:3\n\nimplementability_evidence: Creating a quarterly cross-functional review board and compliance maps requires organizational process changes that are reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\nimplementability:3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Risk-based classification and internal review boards are standard, legally compliant governance practices that offend no norms.\nacceptability:4\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces new classification tools and processes while still treating compliance as a separate logging practice rather than fundamentally changing the lab’s R&D paradigm.\nparadigm_relatedness:2\n\noriginality_evidence: Risk-tiered compliance mapping is a common, mundane response expected from experienced evaluators for regulatory mandates.\noriginality:1\n\nReturn.\n\n",
