king: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nLiving documentation is standard compliance practice? Git-based doc is somewhat new but still treats compliance as documentation. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice? Wait it's documentation not logging. Might be 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious. Living technical documentation protocol, Git-based workflow with automated alert for mandatory review. That's fairly common in software engineering. Not unusual. Maybe originality =1 common mundane boring. Or 2 interesting. I'd say 1 common.\n\nLet's decide scores:\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The proposal focuses on synchronizing technical documentation via Git alerts rather than automatic event logging required by Article 12.\neffectiveness: 1? Could be 1 solves unrelated problem. It would not solve logging mandate even if implemented. So 1.\n\napplicability_evidence: Documentation synchronization addresses traceability generally but does not produce logs of system use, matches, or verification events mandated in Article 12.\napplicability: 2? Maybe 2 may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates. Could be 2.\n\nacceptability_evidence: A Git-based documentation workflow with compliance alerts is a standard engineering practice that violates no legal or business norms.\nacceptability: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Setting up automated alerts on model updates requires modest tooling changes but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\nimplement