g mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. The tool uses logs, assumes logs exist. Might contribute but not fully solve mandate of record-keeping itself. However it transforms passive record-keeping into active tool... Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps but only partial). Could be 4? It doesn't ensure automatic recording as required by Article 12(1). It just scans logs. So it's not solving core logging mandate, just adds value. So 3.\n\nEvidence: The framework assumes compliant logging already exists and adds detection value, so it contributes to traceability and monitoring but does not itself create the mandatory automatic logs.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nThis is acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Building an internal analytics layer on top of required logs is a common, norm-compliant engineering practice with no legal or ethical concerns.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n\nLightweight analysis layer using anomaly detection sitting atop existing logging infrastructure. That's reasonably feasible, maybe some changes. Could be 3. Not trivially easy? Might be easy to implement at low cost? Lightweight... I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Deploying a lightweight anomaly-detection layer requires integration with existing logging pipelines and model tuning, which is feasible but involves non-trivial engineering effort.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nFormat: Probably JSON? Instruction says for each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Might output lines.\n\nLet's produce structured output.\n\nI'll output:\nid: P2503\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
