ain problem is meet logging mandate broadly. This only covers one subclause.\n\nSo effectiveness = 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4? \"will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\". No, it wouldn't solve whole logging mandate. So 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProposal does X = build UI for attestation, get Y = record identification of persons involved in verification. Y is part of stated problem (Article 12(3)(d)). So directly related. Score 4.\n\nNovelty originality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nCommon? Compliance verification UI with digital sign-off for human reviewers is common, mundane. Probably common practice for human-in-the-loop. Not ingenious. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting? Probably common. I'd say 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is a dedicated interface for attestation, lightweight dashboard. That's standard compliance logging practice. Does not change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations beyond logging. Slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Possibly 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged.\n\nPrevent output finalization without signature changes workflow somewhat. Might be slight change. I think it's paradigm preserving? Many labs already have sign-off. I'd lean to 2? The description says introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's level 2. Could be level 1. Hmm.\n\nGiven typical rating, building UI for attestation is standard compliance practice. Probably paradigm preserving =1.\n\nLet's choose 1? Let's think: \"standard compliance and logging practice; the lab's way of working is unchanged\". Actually it does change workflow: prevent output finalization without signature. That's a change. But maybe still within standard.\n\nI'll pick paradigm_relatedness =2 (slightly paradigm stretching). Because introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nWorkability implementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nLightweight dashboard linking model outputs to reviewer IDs, recording timestamp and identity, prevent output finalization. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Lightweight suggests easy. Might be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Building UI is moderate effort. I think 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities =4. Digital sign-off for human verification is acceptable, complies with law. So 4.\n\nNow need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nOrder listed: effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nLet's craft concise one sentence evidence each.\n\nEffectiveness_evidence: The UI only creates an attestation record for human verification of results, leaving other Article 12 logging requirements such as automatic event logs and usage periods unaddressed.\nScore 3.\n\nApplicability_evidence: It directly implements the identification of natural persons involved in verification required by Article 12(3)(d) for specified high-risk systems.\nScore 4.\n\nOriginality_evidence: Human-in-the-loop sign-off dashboards are common compliance practice for regulated AI and are not ingenious or surprising.\nScore 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a logging tool but keeps compliance as separate record-keeping rather than changing how R&D is conducted.\nMaybe choose score 2? If evidence says keeps compliance as separate, that aligns with level 2? Level 2 says introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Hmm.\n\nIf we say paradigm preserving =1, evidence should reflect unchanged way of working. Might be okay.\n\nLet's decide paradigm_relatedness =1 (paradigm preserving). Evidence: It implements standard record-keeping for human attestation without altering the lab's research process or its relationship to compliance obligations.\n\nScore 1.\n\nImplementability_evidence: A lightweight dashboard with reviewer ID linking and signature gating is a reasonably feasible software change for an R&D lab.\nScore 3 (or 4). Might be easy. Let's say 3.\n\nAcceptability_evidence: Digital attestation by human reviewers aligns with legal requirements and does not offend norms or sensibilities.\nScore 4.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably plain text. Instruction says return rating echoing id exactly. Provide each subscale evidence then score.\n\nI'll output structured lines.\n\nProbably format:\nid: P2326\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: